<h3>Cost Component Matrix: Local vs. Intercity</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Base Fare</strong>
<ul>
<li><em>Local Moves:</em> This covers the first 10–15 km of travel plus the standard labor charge for loading and unloading. It is a fixed minimum fee.</li>
<li><em>Intercity Moves:</em> This covers the first 50 km of the total distance and includes basic packing materials. It serves as the foundational tier for long-distance logistics.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Weight Slab</strong>
<ul>
<li><em>Local Moves:</em> Costed per quintal (100 kg) or per truckload. Small moves (under 200 kg) often attract a higher per-kg rate, while full truckloads offer better economies of scale.</li>
<li><em>Intercity Moves:</em> Calculated in strict weight slabs (e.g., 0–300 kg, 300–500 kg). Heavier loads reduce the per-kg cost significantly, making it essential to pack efficiently to optimize this factor.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Distance</strong>
<ul>
<li><em>Local Moves:</em> Charged per kilometer beyond the base inclusion. Traffic conditions and city limits rarely affect the rate, which remains strictly mileage-based.</li>
<li><em>Intercity Moves:</em> Charged per kilometer beyond the first 50 km. Toll taxes, fuel surcharges, and driver night charges are often added at the destination, so clarify these in advance.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Extra Services</strong>
<ul>
<li><em>Local Moves:</em> Stair climbing charges (if no elevator), furniture disassembly, and specialized packaging for fragile items like glassware or electronics.</li>
<li><em>Intercity Moves:</em> Full-service packing, insurance coverage for transit damage, and professional unpacking at the new address. These are optional but highly recommended for peace of mind.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul></div>
